From: Dário Jr
Subject: [PATCH] NVRMFB: publish HiDPI modes on cards with a large BAR1

Base: nullmoth/nvidia-macos-driver @ 7412cdd (release 2.9)
Applies to: kexts/NVRMFB/fb/nvrm-fb.cpp

1. Intercept every write of IOFBMemorySize. getVRAMRange() returns the whole
   BAR1 (8 GB with Resizable BAR on an RTX 5070); a later 32-bit write of that
   length truncates to 0. Zero, or anything above the cap, is published as
   just under 2 GB, which reads the same as a signed/unsigned 32-bit or a
   64-bit number. Each write is logged (first 8).

2. Report the whole scanout allocation as the system aperture instead of the
   current mode's surface only. nvfbhidpi=1 already sizes that allocation for
   a desktop twice the panel; the aperture still covers only memory this
   framebuffer owns.

Tested on: ASRock B550M, Ryzen 5 5600X, RTX 5070 12 GB (GB205), 3440x1440
165 Hz display, macOS 26.7.3, NullMoth driver 2.9, boot-arg nvfbhidpi=1, plus
the display override in ../hidpi. The two changes were tested together; it
was not isolated whether the override alone would have been enough.

Required Notice: Copyright (c) 2026 NullMoth Systems (https://nullmothsystems.com)
License: PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 (see LICENSE in this folder)

---
diff --git a/kexts/NVRMFB/fb/nvrm-fb.cpp b/kexts/NVRMFB/fb/nvrm-fb.cpp
index e900e42..44970b8 100644
--- a/kexts/NVRMFB/fb/nvrm-fb.cpp
+++ b/kexts/NVRMFB/fb/nvrm-fb.cpp
@@ -735,8 +735,31 @@ public:
             if (t != fTransform) { fTransform = t; fNDT = 0; }
             publishRotatedSizes();
         }
+        if (key && key->isEqualTo(kIOFBMemorySizeKey)) {
+            if (OSNumber *n = OSDynamicCast(OSNumber, value)) {
+                const unsigned long long v = n->unsigned64BitValue(), fixed = memorySizeFor(v);
+                if (fMemSizeLogged < 8) { fMemSizeLogged++;
+                    FBLOG("fb%u: IOFBMemorySize write %llu (%u bits) -> %llu", fIndex, v, (unsigned)n->numberOfBits(), fixed); }
+                if (fixed != v) {
+                    OSNumber *f = OSNumber::withNumber(fixed, 32);
+                    if (f) { const bool r = IOFramebuffer::setProperty(key, f); f->release(); return r; }
+                }
+            }
+        }
         return IOFramebuffer::setProperty(key, value);
     }
+    // IOFBMemorySize as WindowServer reads it. The BAR1 this framebuffer reports as VRAM is 4 GB or more with Resizable
+    // BAR (8 GB on an RTX 5070), and a write of that length as a 32-bit number keeps only the low bits: 0. With 0 there,
+    // WindowServer dropped every mode whose desktop is larger than the boot surface, so HiDPI "looks like" sizes that
+    // were validated and installed here never reached the mode list. A zero or larger-than-cap size is published as
+    // just under 2 GB, which reads the same as a signed or unsigned 32-bit or a 64-bit number and is still far more than
+    // the largest scanout surface (7680x4320 at 4 bytes is 127 MB).
+    uint32_t fMemSizeLogged = 0;
+    static unsigned long long memorySizeFor(unsigned long long v)
+    {
+        static const unsigned long long kCap = 2047ull << 20;
+        return (!v || v > kCap) ? kCap : v;
+    }
     void free() override;
     IOReturn enableController() override;
     const char *getPixelFormats() override { return kPixelFormats; }
@@ -5031,6 +5054,11 @@ IODeviceMemory *NVRMNVDAFramebuffer::getApertureRange(IOPixelAperture aperture)
     const NvU64 owned = fConsoleAperture ? fConsSize : fMemBytes;
     IOByteCount bytes = (IOByteCount)nvrmApertureBytes(fPitch, fH, owned);
     if (!bytes) return NULL;
+    // The whole scanout allocation, not only the current mode's surface: nvfbhidpi sizes it for a desktop twice the
+    // panel, and WindowServer leaves out every mode whose surface does not fit the aperture, so with the boot surface
+    // alone (19.9 MB at 3440x1440) every HiDPI desktop (6880x2880 is 79 MB) was validated and installed here but never
+    // listed. The allocation is this framebuffer's own memory, so the aperture still covers nothing else.
+    if (!fConsoleAperture && owned > bytes) bytes = (IOByteCount)owned;
     IOService *nub = pciNub();
     if (nub) {
         IOItemCount n = nub->getDeviceMemoryCount();
